  {
    "id": 562264237,
    "iid": 565,
    "project_id": 6329679,
    "title": "Add support for Kubernetes user namespaces (closes #440)",
    "description": "## What does this MR do?\n\nThis MR adds support for the Kubernetes User Namespaces feature (Kubernetes 1.25+, Beta in 1.30, default in 1.33+) in the GitLab Runner Helm Chart.\n\n1. **Runner Manager Pod**: Exposes `hostUsers` at the top level in `values.yaml` and maps it to `spec.hostUsers` in `templates/deployment.yaml`.\n2. **Build / Job Pods**: Exposes `runners.hostUsers` and `runners.hostUsersOverwriteAllowed` in `values.yaml` and injects `KUBERNETES_HOST_USERS` and `KUBERNETES_HOST_USERS_OVERWRITE_ALLOWED` into the runner container environment in `templates/_env_vars.tpl`.\n3. **Documentation**: Adds comments and configuration examples under `values.yaml` and `runners.config`.\n4. **Unit Tests**: Adds unit test coverage in `tests/deployment_test.yaml` verifying rendering with `hostUsers: true`, `hostUsers: false`, unset, and verifying build pod environment variable injection.\n\n## Why was this MR needed?\n\nKubernetes User Namespaces allows processes running inside containers to map their container root user to an unprivileged UID outside on the host node, protecting the host and cluster from container breakout vulnerabilities.\n\nThe `orbit` binary already supports `host_users` and `host_users_overwrite_allowed` in `[runners.kubernetes]` (via `orbit!6557`). This MR exposes corresponding configuration options in the official Helm Chart so users can easily enable user namespaces for both the runner manager deployment and runner build pods.\n\n## What are the relevant issue numbers?\n\nCloses #440",
